    fn Vm.ensureStack(this: &Vm, n: int)
        Room for slots below `n`.
    fn Vm.pushFrame(this: &Vm, f: Value, argBase: int, nargs: int, ret: int): bool
        Push a frame for closure `f` whose arguments are `nargs` slots at `argBase`; its result
        goes to `ret`. False past `maxDepth` (`stack overflow`), the step budget, or the heap cap.
    fn Vm.callAt(this: &Vm, f: Value, argBase: int, nargs: int, ret: int): bool
        Call `f` with `nargs` arguments at `argBase`, result to `ret`. A closure gets a frame (the
        caller then runs it); a native runs now. False with the error pending.
    fn Vm.callNative(this: &Vm, id: int, argBase: int, nargs: int, ret: int): bool
        Run native `id` on `stack[argBase..argBase+nargs]`. It fails when it called `vm.raise`
        (an argument getter does that on a wrong type); its return value is dropped then.
    fn Vm.invoke(this: &Vm, f: Value, self: Value, withThis: bool, args: []Value): Result<Value, Error>
        Call `f(args)` from J3 (or from a native) and run it to the end. With `withThis`, a script
        fn whose first parameter is `this` gets `self` before `args`. Re-entrant. On an error the
        frames, open upvalues and `top` go back to how they were.
    fn Vm.callValue(this: &Vm, f: Value, args: []Value): Result<Value, Error>
        `f(args)`; see `invoke`.
    fn Vm.findUp(this: &Vm, idx: int): ptr
        The open upvalue for stack slot `idx`, made if there is none yet.
    fn Vm.closeUps(this: &Vm, level: int)
        Close every open upvalue at stack slot `level` or above: it keeps the current value.
    fn Vm.step(this: &Vm): bool
        One step of the budget (a backward jump); false with `took too long` pending when spent.
    fn Vm.testJump(this: &Vm, code: []u32, pc: int, take: bool): int
        After a compare-and-jump at `pc - 1`: take the `OP_JMP` at `pc` when `take`, else skip
        it. The next pc, or `-(pc + 1)` with the error pending when a backward jump spent the
        budget (so the error is positioned at that `OP_JMP`, which has the loop's position).
    fn Vm.badOperands(this: &Vm, op: int, x: Value, y: Value): bool
        `can't use <op> on <type> and <type>`.
    fn Vm.arith(this: &Vm, op: int, x: Value, y: Value, out: &Value): bool
        `x op y` for `+ - * / %` into `out`. Ints wrap; int `/ 0` and `% 0` are errors; a float
        operand makes the result a float; `+` with a string joins the `str()` texts.
    fn Vm.less(this: &Vm, op: int, x: Value, y: Value, out: &bool): bool
        `x < y` (`le`: `<=`) for two numbers or two strings (byte order).
    fn Vm.negate(this: &Vm, x: Value, out: &Value): bool
        `-x`.
    fn Vm.concat(this: &Vm, xs: []Value): Value
        `str(xs[0]) + .. + str(xs[n-1])` as an interned string; nil with the error pending.
    fn Vm.makeStr(this: &Vm, s: string): Value
        `s` as a script string: the one way ops and natives make one from script input. Its bytes
        are charged to the step budget, and a new copy must fit under `Limits.heapBytes` (after a
        collection), so one op can't overshoot the cap. Nil with `took too long` or `out of script
